course: biology
lesson: 244
title: Animal builders: spiders' webs and silk
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: On an autumn morning a garden fence can glitter with a wheel of dew drops: that is an orb web, usually almost invisible.
explain: The spider spins it from silk that comes out of its body as a liquid and hardens as it is pulled out of little nozzles called spinnerets at the back of its body.
explain: The web is a wheel of dry spokes with a sticky spiral, and the spider sits waiting, feeling the threads the way you feel a phone buzz in your pocket.
explain: Not every spider builds a web: some dig trapdoors, some live in an air bubble under a pond, some swing a sticky ball, and some hunt like little cats with big eyes.
explain: For its weight, some spider silk is stronger than steel and very stretchy, which is why scientists study it so closely.
fact: An orb web is a wheel of strong spokes with a sticky spiral that catches flying insects; dew on the silk shows the pattern, and a garden spider often eats its old web and builds a new one nearly every day.
fact: The spider feels vibrations through the threads and can tell where the prey is and how big it is; many web spiders see poorly and read the web like a telephone line.
fact: The spider walks mostly on the dry spokes and has special claws and hairs on its feet; only the spiral carries sticky glue drops.
fact: For its weight, some spider silk is stronger than steel and very stretchy, because its proteins are lined up in a special way; Darwin's bark spider in Madagascar spins webs across rivers up to about 25 metres wide.
fact: Many young spiders let out a thread and float away on the wind, which is called ballooning; experiments suggest the Earth's electric field may help to lift them, and they have landed on ships far out at sea.
fact: Trapdoor spiders dig burrows with a hinged lid of soil and silk, feel insects walk by through trip lines of silk, and some live over 40 years.
fact: The diving bell spider makes a silk tent under water and fills it with air carried from the surface; it is the only spider that lives almost all its life under water.
fact: Real spiders cannot write words, but some weave zigzag silk decorations into their webs, which may warn birds away or attract insects; a spider can make up to seven kinds of silk.
fact: The bolas spider makes no web: it swings a single line with a sticky blob that smells like a female moth, and male moths fly in and get caught.
fact: Jumping spiders build no catching web: they stalk and leap, their big front eyes work like telescopes, they see colours, and some plan a route round obstacles to reach their prey.
fact: There is no evidence that people swallow spiders in their sleep; a sleeping person breathes, snores and moves, which spiders avoid, and the «fact» spread without proof.
fact: All spiders have eight legs, and most house spiders are harmless and eat flies and mosquitoes.
exercise: What makes a spider's web suddenly easy to see on an autumn morning? | Drops of dew stuck on the silk | The web turns white in the cold | The spider paints it with spit | The sun burns the web silver
exercise: What does a garden spider often do with its old web? | Eats it and recycles the silk for a new one | Leaves it hanging empty in the bushes for years and years | Gives it to its babies as a bed | Sells it to other spiders
exercise: How does a web spider know that a fly has been caught? | It feels the vibrations through the threads | It sees the fly coming with its sharp eyes | It hears the fly buzzing from a long way off | It smells the fly from the next garden
exercise: Which part of an orb web is sticky? | Only the spiral | Only the long spokes | Every single thread | None of it at all
exercise: Why does a spider not get stuck on its own web? | It walks mostly on dry spokes and has special claws and hairs | It is far too light to stick, so the glue cannot hold it at all | It covers itself in oil every morning | It never touches its own web
exercise: How does a spider's silk start out? | As a liquid that hardens as it is pulled out | As hairs that slowly fall off its back and twist together | As a powder blown out of its mouth | As thin strips cut from leaves
exercise: What is true about the strength of spider silk? | For its weight, some is stronger than steel | It is the weakest thread anywhere in the whole of nature | It is exactly as strong as wool | It snaps if a fly touches it
exercise: Which spider spins webs across rivers up to about 25 metres wide? | Darwin's bark spider | The common garden spider | The little jumping spider | The night-hunting bolas spider
exercise: What is spider ballooning? | Young spiders floating away on silk threads in the air | Spiders puffing themselves up like balloons when they are scared | Spiders living inside balloons at parties | Spiders floating on leaves down rivers
exercise: How does a trapdoor spider know an insect is walking past? | Silk trip lines round its door shake | It peeps out of the door all day and night long | It listens with the little ears on its legs | The insect knocks on the lid of its door
exercise: How does the diving bell spider breathe under water? | It lives in a silk tent filled with air from the surface | It has gills like a fish | It holds its breath for a whole year without coming up for air | It breathes through a reed straw
exercise: What do zigzag silk lines in some webs probably do? | Warn birds away or attract insects | Spell out messages to other spiders | Show how old the spider is in years | Hold the web up in strong storms only
exercise: How does a bolas spider catch male moths? | It swings a sticky blob that smells like a female moth | It spins the biggest web in the world | It chases them through the air at night, catching them on the wing | It hides in flowers and grabs them
exercise: How does a jumping spider catch its food? | It stalks it with big sharp eyes and leaps | It waits in a huge sticky web for days and days | It digs a pit and waits at the bottom | It spits glue from far away
exercise: Do people swallow spiders in their sleep? | There is no evidence they do, spiders avoid a breathing, moving person | Yes, about eight every year, mostly on cold winter nights when we snore | Yes, but only in summer | Only if they sleep with their mouths open
exercise: How many kinds of silk can one spider make? | Up to about seven | Only one single kind | Exactly two kinds, no more | Over a hundred kinds
test: In Charlotte's Web the spider spins words like «Some Pig» into her web. What can real spiders do? | They cannot write, but some weave zigzag decorations into their webs | They write their names in silk | They copy the letters and words they see written on signs and posters | They can only spin straight lines
test: «spidey shoots webs out his wrists innit, real spiders do that too right» What is right? | No, silk comes from spinnerets at the back of the body | Yes, from the tips of their front legs, like threads from a reel | Yes, from their mouths | No, real spiders cannot make silk at all
test: «3 spiders in the bath, how many legs, quick» | 24 | 18 | 12 | 30
test: «there were 4 spiders in the shed, no wait 5. legs?» | 40 | 32 | 20 | 45
test: «4 spiders and 2 flies in the web. how many legs altogether» | 44 | 40 | 36 | 48
test: «my sis says we eat 8 spiders a year in our sleep, gross» What is true? | There is no evidence for it, it spread without proof | It is true, scientists counted them while people slept in labs | It is true, but only for children | It is true for spiders but not insects
test: What would happen if a spider walked across the sticky spiral of its own web carelessly? | It could get stuck, which is why it keeps to the dry spokes | Nothing at all, because every thread of the whole web is dry | The web would turn to water | The spider would sink through the web
test: A tiny spider rises into the air on a still autumn day with no wind. What might be helping it? | The Earth's electric field pulling on its silk | A bird carrying it in its beak for miles | Its tiny hidden wings flapping very fast | Warm water underneath it
test: Why is spider silk useful to the spider in a sticky web and a stretchy line both? | The spider makes different kinds of silk for different jobs | All silk is exactly the same | The spider buys silk from other spiders | Silk changes by itself with the weather
test: «that jumpy spider is looking right at me with them big eyes. creepy» What is right? | Jumping spiders really do see well and judge distance with their big front eyes | Spiders are completely blind, so those eyes cannot see anything at all | Those are fake eyes painted on its back | It is only looking at the light behind you
test: A trapdoor spider in a zoo is 40 years old. Is that possible? | Yes, some trapdoor spiders live over 40 years | No, every spider dies before its first birthday | No, spiders live only a few weeks, even in zoos | Yes, but only if it sleeps through every winter
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the structure of an orb web? | It consists of dry radial threads supporting an adhesive spiral | It is composed throughout of uniformly adhesive silk | It possesses no adhesive element whatsoever | It is constructed of fibres collected from plants
exam: Identify the principal means by which a web-building spider ascertains the capture of its prey. | The perception of vibrations transmitted through the threads | Continuous visual observation at great distance | The detection of sound carried through the air | The assistance of other spiders stationed nearby
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the popular assertion that persons swallow spiders whilst asleep. | No evidence supports it, and it was disseminated without proof | It has been confirmed by extensive scientific measurement | It occurs solely during the winter months | It is restricted to persons residing near forests
exam: Ascertain the manner in which the diving bell spider obtains air whilst living beneath the surface of a pond. | It maintains a tent of silk replenished with air conveyed from the surface | It extracts air from the water through gills in the manner of a fish | It abstains from breathing for the duration of its life | It respires through a hollow plant stem fixed above the water at the surface
